// std/sysproc.jtr — a child process that runs BESIDE its parent.
//
// TIER: `sys`. Needs a process table. Capability-gated through a `Spawner`, the same
// shape `std/process` uses and for the same reason.
//
// ─── Why this exists when `std/process` already runs commands ─────────────────
//
// `std/process` goes through the `run_command` intrinsic, which is `system()`, and
// **`system()` blocks until the child exits.** That single fact is the ceiling under
// everything above it:
//
//   * **No timeouts, anywhere.** A hung child hangs the parent, with no way to give up.
//     `std/plugin`'s host cannot bound a wedged plugin and `jserve` cannot bound a slow
//     client, because neither has a call that returns before the child does.
//   * **No long-lived server.** `std/plugin` is one-process-per-call — not a design
//     choice, a consequence: the only way to get an answer back is to let the child die.
//   * **No concurrent loopback child.** The socket design `std/sysnet` would otherwise
//     support needs a child running WHILE its parent is in `accept`, which is precisely
//     what `system()` forbids.
//
// Nothing here replaces `std/process`. That module is the right answer when you want a
// command run and its exit code, which is most of the time; this one is for when the
// parent has to stay alive and in charge while the child runs.
//
// ─── The command means the same thing it means in `std/process` ───────────────
//
// A command goes through the platform SHELL — `/bin/sh -c <cmd>` on POSIX,
// `cmd.exe /c <cmd>` on Windows — which is exactly what `system()` does on each. That is
// a deliberate compatibility choice rather than a convenience: a caller moving a command
// from `process.run` to `start` here must not have to re-learn how its own string is
// parsed. The cost is the shell's quoting rules, and they are the platform's, not this
// module's.
//
// **This is not a sandbox**, for the same reason `std/process` says so: `run_command` is
// still an ambient intrinsic that any function can reach. What a `Spawner` buys is that
// the honest path is also the documented one — `fn build(mut s: Spawner, …)` announces in
// its type that it may start processes, and `fn parse(read src: str)` announces that it
// may not.
//
// ─── `GetExitCodeProcess` CANNOT answer "is it still running" ─────────────────
//
// The obvious Windows liveness test is to ask for the exit code and compare against
// `STILL_ACTIVE`. It is wrong, and it is wrong in a way that testing rarely catches:
// `STILL_ACTIVE` is **259**, and a child that legitimately calls `exit(259)` is then
// reported as running forever. Measured against the real header rather than recalled —
// `the_windows_layout_constants_match_the_real_headers` pins it along with the struct
// offsets.
//
// The correct test is `WaitForSingleObject(h, 0)`: it reports SIGNALLED or TIMEOUT and
// has no in-band value to collide with. The exit code is read only AFTER that says the
// process is done, which is the one moment `STILL_ACTIVE` cannot be ambiguous.
//
// ─── The bounded wait is a POLL on both platforms, on purpose ─────────────────
//
// Windows has a native bounded wait and POSIX has none — `waitpid` blocks or returns
// immediately (`WNOHANG`) and there is nothing in between. The two ways to build one on
// POSIX are `sigtimedwait` on `SIGCHLD`, which needs the signal blocked process-wide and
// so makes a library reach into state it does not own, or a poll.
//
// So `wait_timeout` polls on BOTH platforms, even though Windows could pass the timeout
// straight to `WaitForSingleObject`. The reason is that the loop is then the same code
// on both, driven by the same `time.Clock`, so a test of the timeout LOGIC exercises the
// shipped path everywhere instead of testing Windows and reasoning about POSIX. Only two
// primitives are `@cfg`-split — "is it alive?" and "what did it exit with?" — and the
// policy above them is written once.
//
// `wait` (unbounded) does NOT poll: it hands off to the platform's own blocking wait,
// because there a poll would be a busy loop with nothing to gain.
//
// ─── A `Child` is a RESOURCE, and `@move` is how the language says so ─────────
//
// `Child` holds an OS handle, so `release`-ing through a COPY would leave the other name
// pointing at a handle the platform may already have reissued. It is `@move`, which makes
// that a compile error: rebinding a `Child` or giving one to a `take` parameter consumes
// it, and reading the old name is refused.
//
// It is NOT droppable, and that is the other half of the design. Brief §2.1's recorded
// obstacle was that a `Drop` closes at every scope exit, which is wrong for a handle
// deliberately passed around — so `@move` grants "may not be duplicated" WITHOUT "runs
// teardown automatically". Only the first property is unsound; the second would also have
// discarded the verdict of a fallible close.
//
// The remaining invariant is a caller's: `release` exactly once, and `is_live` reports
// whether that has happened. On Windows a released `Child` leaks nothing — `release` closes
// both the process and thread handles, and forgetting the thread handle is the classic
// `CreateProcess` leak.
//
// ─── `spawn` is a KEYWORD, so the central verb here is `start` ────────────────
//
// `spawn f(x)` is the concurrency grammar's task form, so `pub fn spawn` does not parse.
// That is a fifth entry on the recorded list (`read`, `take`, `error`, `out`) and the
// first one the declared-extern-alias design would NOT fix: this is a Jestyr name, not a
// C symbol, so the fix is scoped keywords or nothing.
//
// ─── PIPES: the child can be talked to, not just started ─────────────────────
//
// `start_piped` connects the child's stdin and stdout to this process, so a parent can
// send a request and read the answer while the child stays alive. That is the shape a
// long-lived plugin server needs, and the one `system()` made impossible — `std/plugin`
// is one-process-per-call because the only way to get an answer back was to let the child
// die.
//
// **The deadlock is the thing to understand, and it is not hypothetical.** A pipe's buffer
// is finite (4KB on Linux, ~64KB on Windows). A child that outgrows it blocks in `write`
// until somebody drains the other end; a parent sitting in `wait` never will. Neither side
// is wrong and the program simply stops. So: `close_input`, then drain to EOF, THEN `wait`.
// `capture` is those first two in one call, which is why it exists — the safe order should
// be the short thing to write.
//
// stderr is merged into the stdout pipe on purpose: two pipes are two things to drain, and
// draining two from one thread without a poll is the same deadlock with extra steps.
//
// ─── What is still not here ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// No environment control, no working directory, no process groups. Each is a real thing
// to want and none of them is needed to lift the ceiling this module exists to lift.
//
// **The environment a child inherits is now the SAME on both platforms** (see `env_block`
// at the `posix_spawn` call). What follows is the difference as it stood, kept because the
// reasoning is why the fix is what it is rather than a null on both sides.
//
// ~~**The environment a child inherits is NOT the same on both platforms**~~, and that is
// stated rather than smoothed over: a Windows child gets its parent's whole environment
// (`CreateProcessA` with a null environment inherits), a POSIX child gets `PATH` alone
// (`posix_spawn` with a null `envp` would give it NOTHING, since that reaches `execve`
// unchanged). See `start`'s POSIX branch. Closing the gap needs an `extern` that can bind
// a GLOBAL — `environ` — which is a language increment, so it is recorded here instead of
// being papered over with a null on both sides that means two different things.
//
// Worked example:  examples/std/sysproc_demo.jtr
// Run the tests:   jestyrc test examples/std/sysproc_test.jtr

import "syserr"
import "time"

// ---- the platform ------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// POSIX and Windows names are all distinct here, so unlike `std/sysnet` there is no
// same-name pair to unify. The one binding that MUST match another module exactly is
// `WaitForSingleObject`: `std/syswatch` already declares it, typeck keys its function
// table on the BARE name, and a second declaration with a different signature would type
// -check `syswatch`'s call sites against THIS module's idea of it. Same spelling, same
// types, deliberately.
//
// The pin is `extern_signature_agreement::one_c_symbol_has_one_signature_across_the_whole
// _std_corpus`, which sweeps EVERY extern in `examples/std` rather than this one pair, so
// the next module to re-bind a shared symbol is caught without anyone remembering to look.
// It was verified by BREAKING it — widening this line to `(h: i64, ms: i32)` and watching
// the sweep name both files — because a corpus-wide rule that passes on its first run has
// not yet been shown to be capable of failing.

@cfg(posix) extern "spawn.h" fn posix_spawn(pid: cptr, path: cptr, fa: cptr, at: cptr, argv: cptr, envp: cptr) -> i32
@cfg(posix) extern "sys/wait.h" fn waitpid(pid: i64, status: cptr, options: i32) -> i64
@cfg(posix) extern "signal.h" fn kill(pid: i64, sig: i32) -> i32

// The piped path (see `start_piped`). `fork`+`exec` rather than `posix_spawn`, because
// redirecting a child's descriptors through `posix_spawn` needs a
// `posix_spawn_file_actions_t` — an OPAQUE struct whose size differs between libcs (80
// bytes on glibc, a bare pointer on macOS). Allocating a guessed number of bytes for it
// would be exactly the layout claim `std/sysdir`'s header refuses to make. `fork`/`dup2`/
// `execv` need no layout at all, and everything the child does between the two is
// async-signal-safe, which is the rule that makes `fork` without `exec` dangerous and
// `fork` immediately followed by `exec` fine.
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn pipe(fds: cptr) -> i32
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn fork() -> i64
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn dup2(oldfd: i64, newfd: i64) -> i32
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn execv(path: cptr, argv: cptr) -> i32

// **Three declared aliases, for three different reasons — this module is the first real
// consumer of that feature.**
//
//   * `read` is a Jestyr KEYWORD (the `read x: T` parameter mode), so `extern fn read`
//     does not parse at all. This is the case the alias was built for; `std/syswatch`
//     works around it by binding `readv` and driving it with a one-element iovec.
//   * `_exit` starts with an underscore, which is a fine C symbol and an awkward Jestyr
//     name.
//   * `close` is neither — but `std/sysnet` and `std/syswatch` already bind it, and typeck
//     keys its function table on the BARE name. Aliasing gives this module its own name
//     for the same symbol instead of a fourth declaration that has to agree with three
//     others forever.
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn sys_read = "read"(fd: i64, buf: cptr, n: usize) -> i64
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn sys_write = "write"(fd: i64, buf: cptr, n: usize) -> i64
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn sys_close = "close"(fd: i64) -> i32
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn sys_exit = "_exit"(code: i32)

@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn CreateProcessA(app: cptr, cmd: cptr, pa: cptr, ta: cptr, inherit: i32, flags: i32, env: cptr, cwd: cptr, si: cptr, pi: cptr) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn WaitForSingleObject(h: cptr, ms: u32) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn GetExitCodeProcess(h: cptr, code: cptr) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn TerminateProcess(h: cptr, code: u32) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn CloseHandle(h: cptr) -> i32

// The piped path. `CreatePipe` hands back an inheritable pair; `SetHandleInformation`
// then makes the PARENT's end non-inheritable, which is the step everyone forgets — a
// child that inherits the read end of its own stdout pipe keeps that pipe open, so the
// parent's read never sees EOF and a drain loop hangs forever.
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn CreatePipe(rd: cptr, wr: cptr, sa: cptr, size: u32) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn SetHandleInformation(h: cptr, mask: u32, flags: u32) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn ReadFile(h: cptr, buf: cptr, n: u32, got: cptr, ov: cptr) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn WriteFile(h: cptr, buf: cptr, n: u32, put: cptr, ov: cptr) -> i32

// ---- the numbers -------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every Windows constant below was MEASURED against <windows.h> on x86-64 rather than
// recalled, and `the_windows_layout_constants_match_the_real_headers` re-measures them so
// a wrong number is a red test and not a mysterious failure to start anything.
// (Prefixed because a module `const` is emitted under its bare name, so two modules
// cannot share one — `std/process` already owns `PROC_`.)

// STARTUPINFOA on x86-64: 104 bytes, `cb` first.
const SPROC_STARTUPINFO_LEN: usize = 104
// PROCESS_INFORMATION on x86-64: hProcess, hThread, dwProcessId, dwThreadId.
const SPROC_PROCESS_INFO_LEN: usize = 24
const SPROC_PI_HPROCESS_SLOT: usize = 0     // byte 0,  read as a 64-bit slot
const SPROC_PI_HTHREAD_SLOT: usize = 1      // byte 8
const SPROC_PI_PID_WORD: usize = 4          // byte 16, read as a 32-bit word

const SPROC_WAIT_SIGNALED: i32 = 0          // WAIT_OBJECT_0
const SPROC_WAIT_TIMEOUT: i32 = 258         // WAIT_TIMEOUT
const SPROC_WAIT_FAILED: i32 = 0 - 1        // WAIT_FAILED is 0xFFFFFFFF
const SPROC_KILL_CODE: u32 = 137            // what a terminated child is reported as

// POSIX. `waitpid`'s status is the classic encoding: the low seven bits are the signal
// that killed it (zero if it exited), and the next eight are the exit code. Linux and
// macOS agree on this layout; it is decoded here rather than through `WIFEXITED` because
// those are MACROS, and a macro is not a symbol an `extern` can bind.
const SPROC_WNOHANG: i32 = 1
const SPROC_SIGKILL: i32 = 9
const SPROC_STATUS_SIGMASK: i32 = 127
const SPROC_STATUS_CODESHIFT: i32 = 8
const SPROC_STATUS_CODEMASK: i32 = 255

// How long a bounded wait sleeps between liveness checks. One millisecond is below the
// granularity either platform's sleep actually offers (Windows' default timer is ~15ms),
// so this is a floor and not a promise — `wait_timeout` reports the elapsed time it
// really observed rather than the one it asked for.
const SPROC_POLL_NANOS: i64 = 1000000

// ---- pipes ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// "No pipe" is **-1 on both platforms**, and that is a coincidence worth stating rather
// than relying on quietly: a POSIX descriptor is a small non-negative int (0 is a perfectly
// good fd — stdin), and a Windows `HANDLE` is never `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE`, which is -1.
// So -1 is unusable as a real value on either, and one sentinel serves both. Pinned by
// `the_windows_pipe_constants_match_the_real_headers` along with everything below it.
const SPROC_NO_PIPE: i64 = 0 - 1

// SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES on x86-64: 24 bytes, `{ DWORD nLength; void* lpSD; BOOL bInherit; }`.
// `bInheritHandle` must be TRUE for `CreatePipe` to hand back handles a child can inherit.
const SPROC_SA_LEN: usize = 24
const SPROC_SA_INHERIT_WORD: usize = 4      // byte 16, read as a 32-bit word

// STARTUPINFOA fields the piped path fills in — measured, like the rest of that struct.
const SPROC_SI_FLAGS_WORD: usize = 15       // byte 60
const SPROC_SI_STDIN_SLOT: usize = 10       // byte 80,  read as a 64-bit slot
const SPROC_SI_STDOUT_SLOT: usize = 11      // byte 88
const SPROC_SI_STDERR_SLOT: usize = 12      // byte 96
const SPROC_STARTF_USESTDHANDLES: u32 = 256
const SPROC_HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT: u32 = 1

// What a Windows read reports when the child has closed its end. It is an ERROR return
// with this code, not a zero-length read, so EOF has to be recognised rather than fallen
// into — and a caller that treated it as a failure would turn every successful capture
// into an error at the last step.
const SPROC_ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE: i32 = 109

// How much `capture` reads at a time. A pipe's own buffer is 4–64KB depending on platform,
// so this is a chunk size and not a limit — `capture` loops.
const SPROC_PIPE_CHUNK: usize = 4096

// ---- what a child is doing ---------------------------------------------------------

// It has not exited.
pub const SPROC_RUNNING: i32 = 0
// It exited normally; `code` is its exit status.
pub const SPROC_EXITED: i32 = 1
// It was killed by a signal (POSIX) or terminated (Windows); `code` is the signal.
pub const SPROC_SIGNALLED: i32 = 2

// The answer to "what is it doing", as one value rather than two out-parameters.
//
// A `state` plus a `code` rather than a single integer with reserved sentinels, because
// the sentinel design is what made `std/process.run` document `-1` as "did not exit
// normally" and then hope no command ever returns it. Here "still running" and "exited
// with 259" are different STATES and cannot be confused whatever the number is.
pub struct Status {
    state: i32,
    code: i32,
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn state_of(read s: Status) -> i32 { return s.state }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn code_of(read s: Status) -> i32 { return s.code }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn is_running(read s: Status) -> bool { return s.state == SPROC_RUNNING }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn is_exited(read s: Status) -> bool { return s.state == SPROC_EXITED }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn is_signalled(read s: Status) -> bool { return s.state == SPROC_SIGNALLED }

// A human-readable state, for a log line or a diagnostic.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn state_name(state: i32) -> str {
    if state == SPROC_RUNNING { return "running" }
    if state == SPROC_EXITED { return "exited" }
    if state == SPROC_SIGNALLED { return "signalled" }
    return "unknown"
}

// ---- the capability ----------------------------------------------------------------

// The authority to start processes. Scalars only: a Jestyr borrow is second-class, so a
// handle cannot own borrowed storage — the same constraint that shapes `std/process`.
pub struct Spawner {
    allowed: bool,
    started: i64,
    refused: i64,
}

// A handle that can actually start processes. Named `host` rather than `new` because
// acquiring the authority to run other programs should read like an act.
pub fn host() -> Spawner {
    return Spawner{ allowed: true, started: 0, refused: 0 }
}

// A handle that starts nothing and COUNTS every attempt, so a test can hand one to code
// under test and afterwards assert both that nothing ran and that the right number of
// attempts were made.
pub fn denied() -> Spawner {
    return Spawner{ allowed: false, started: 0, refused: 0 }
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn can_start(read s: Spawner) -> bool { return s.allowed }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn started(read s: Spawner) -> i64 { return s.started }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn refused(read s: Spawner) -> i64 { return s.refused }

// ---- the child ---------------------------------------------------------------------

// A running (or finished) child process.
//
// `h`/`th` are Windows HANDLEs carried as integers — the one pointer currency this
// module uses, since `i64 as cptr` converts and `i64 as *mut u8` does not. On POSIX both
// are zero and `pid` is the identity.
@move pub struct Child {
    h: i64,
    th: i64,
    pid: i64,
    live: bool,
    done: bool,
    state: i32,
    code: i32,
    // The parent's ends of the child's stdin and stdout, or `SPROC_NO_PIPE`. `start`
    // leaves both absent; `start_piped` fills both in. Closed by `release`.
    inp: i64,
    outp: i64,
}

// A `Child` that names nothing. What `denied()` produces, and what a caller can compare
// against without a separate optional.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn no_child() -> Child {
    return Child{ h: 0, th: 0, pid: 0, live: false, done: true, state: SPROC_EXITED, code: 0, inp: SPROC_NO_PIPE, outp: SPROC_NO_PIPE }
}

// Is this child's handle still ours? False before it started and after `release`.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn is_live(read c: Child) -> bool { return c.live }

// The OS process id. Informational on Windows, the identity on POSIX.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn pid_of(read c: Child) -> i64 { return c.pid }

// The status already known WITHOUT asking the OS again — `SPROC_RUNNING` until one of the
// waits has observed an exit. Cheap, and the only query that is safe after `release`.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn last_status(read c: Child) -> Status {
    if c.done { return Status{ state: c.state, code: c.code } }
    return Status{ state: SPROC_RUNNING, code: 0 }
}

// ---- building the argument buffers --------------------------------------------------

// Copy `s` into a fresh NUL-terminated heap buffer.
//
// Not `cstring.from_str`: that hands back a `CString` whose pointer is reachable only as
// a `cstr`, and POSIX `argv` needs the ADDRESS as an integer to write into an array of
// pointers. One currency (`*mut u8`, cast to `i64` or `cptr` at the call) is simpler than
// two that cannot convert to each other.
fn nul_copy(read s: str) -> *mut u8 {
    var p: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, s.len + 1)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < s.len {
        unsafe { (p + i).* = s[i] }
        i = i + 1
    }
    unsafe { (p + s.len).* = 0 }
    return p
}

// `prefix` followed by `s`, NUL-terminated. The Windows command line is
// `cmd.exe /c <cmd>` and CreateProcessA may WRITE to the buffer it is given, so this is
// a fresh heap copy rather than any view of the caller's string.
fn nul_join(read prefix: str, read s: str) -> *mut u8 {
    var p: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, prefix.len + s.len + 1)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < prefix.len {
        unsafe { (p + i).* = prefix[i] }
        i = i + 1
    }
    var k: usize = 0
    for k < s.len {
        unsafe { (p + prefix.len + k).* = s[k] }
        k = k + 1
    }
    unsafe { (p + prefix.len + s.len).* = 0 }
    return p
}

// Zero `n` bytes. `alloc` is `malloc`, not `calloc`, and a STARTUPINFOA with uninitialised
// `dwFlags` tells CreateProcess to read std-handle fields that hold garbage.
fn zero_bytes(p: *mut u8, n: usize) {
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < n {
        unsafe { (p + i).* = 0 }
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// ---- starting ----------------------------------------------------------------------

// Start `cmd` through the platform shell and return WITHOUT waiting for it.
//
// This is the whole point of the module: when this returns, the child is running and so
// is the caller. A denied `Spawner` starts nothing, counts the attempt, and returns
// `SysProcRefused` — distinguishable from a real failure, which carries the platform's
// own error number.
@cfg(posix) pub fn start(mut s: Spawner, read cmd: str) -> Child !{ SysProcFailed(i32), SysProcRefused } {
    if s.allowed == false {
        s.refused = s.refused + 1
        return err(SysProcRefused)
    }

    // argv = { "sh", "-c", cmd, NULL }. `posix_spawn` takes `char* const argv[]`, which
    // is an array of POINTERS — written here as four 64-bit slots, since a pointer is
    // eight bytes on every platform this module builds for.
    var shp: *mut u8 = nul_copy("/bin/sh")
    var a0: *mut u8 = nul_copy("sh")
    var a1: *mut u8 = nul_copy("-c")
    var a2: *mut u8 = nul_copy(cmd)
    var argv: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, 4)
    unsafe { (argv + 0).* = a0 as i64 }
    unsafe { (argv + 1).* = a1 as i64 }
    unsafe { (argv + 2).* = a2 as i64 }
    unsafe { (argv + 3).* = 0 }

    var pidp: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, 1)
    unsafe { (pidp + 0).* = 0 }

    // **envp = { "PATH=…", NULL }, and NOT NULL.**
    //
    // The two platforms disagree about what a null environment means, and letting that
    // through would have made one module behave two ways. `CreateProcessA` with a NULL
    // environment INHERITS the parent's; `posix_spawn` with a NULL `envp` hands the child
    // an EMPTY one, because it reaches `execve` unchanged. So the obvious "pass null on
    // both" is not symmetry, it is an asymmetry that happens to typecheck.
    //
    // **CLOSED.** This used to forward `PATH` alone, because full inheritance needs the
    // `environ` global and an `extern` binds FUNCTIONS. The header said the fix was
    // "either an `extern` for a global or an intrinsic that enumerates the environment" —
    // it is the second: `env_block()` hands back the `char**` the C runtime already has,
    // with the `extern char** environ` declaration living in the emitted C where the
    // standard puts it.
    //
    // So the two platforms now agree on the OUTCOME rather than merely on the call: a
    // child gets its parent's whole environment on both. Windows still passes null,
    // because null is what `CreateProcessA` reads as "inherit" — the asymmetry was never
    // that null meant nothing, it was that null meant two different things.
    //
    // `start_piped` never had this gap: it uses `fork`+`execv`, and `execv` passes the
    // caller's `environ` by definition. Only this `posix_spawn` path had to say it aloud.
    let rc: i32 = posix_spawn(pidp as cptr, shp as cptr, null, null, argv as cptr, env_block())
    var pid: i64 = 0
    unsafe { pid = (pidp + 0).* }
    free_ptr(pidp)
    free_ptr(argv)
    free_ptr(a2)
    free_ptr(a1)
    free_ptr(a0)
    free_ptr(shp)

    // `posix_spawn` RETURNS the error number; it does not set errno. That is the one way
    // it differs from every other call in the `sys` tier, and reading `syserr.last_raw()`
    // here would report an unrelated earlier failure.
    if rc != 0 { return err(SysProcFailed(rc)) }

    s.started = s.started + 1
    return ok(Child{ h: 0, th: 0, pid: pid, live: true, done: false, state: SPROC_RUNNING, code: 0, inp: SPROC_NO_PIPE, outp: SPROC_NO_PIPE })
}

@cfg(windows) pub fn start(mut s: Spawner, read cmd: str) -> Child !{ SysProcFailed(i32), SysProcRefused } {
    if s.allowed == false {
        s.refused = s.refused + 1
        return err(SysProcRefused)
    }

    var line: *mut u8 = nul_join("cmd.exe /c ", cmd)
    var si: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, SPROC_STARTUPINFO_LEN)
    var pi: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, SPROC_PROCESS_INFO_LEN)
    zero_bytes(si, SPROC_STARTUPINFO_LEN)
    zero_bytes(pi, SPROC_PROCESS_INFO_LEN)
    // `cb` is the first field and CreateProcess rejects a STARTUPINFOA whose size it does
    // not recognise, which is the API's version check.
    let sw: *mut u32 = si as *mut u32
    unsafe { (sw + 0).* = SPROC_STARTUPINFO_LEN as u32 }

    // `lpApplicationName` NULL with a command line means "parse the first token as the
    // program", which is how `cmd.exe` is found on PATH.
    let rc: i32 = CreateProcessA(null, line as cptr, null, null, 0, 0, null, null, si as cptr, pi as cptr)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if rc == 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }

    var h: i64 = 0
    var th: i64 = 0
    var pid: i64 = 0
    if rc != 0 {
        let ps: *mut i64 = pi as *mut i64
        unsafe { h = (ps + SPROC_PI_HPROCESS_SLOT).* }
        unsafe { th = (ps + SPROC_PI_HTHREAD_SLOT).* }
        let pw: *mut u32 = pi as *mut u32
        var raw: u32 = 0
        unsafe { raw = (pw + SPROC_PI_PID_WORD).* }
        pid = raw as i64
    }

    free_ptr(pi)
    free_ptr(si)
    free_ptr(line)

    if rc == 0 { return err(SysProcFailed(code)) }

    s.started = s.started + 1
    return ok(Child{ h: h, th: th, pid: pid, live: true, done: false, state: SPROC_RUNNING, code: 0, inp: SPROC_NO_PIPE, outp: SPROC_NO_PIPE })
}

// ---- pipes: talking to the child, not just starting it -------------------------------
//
// **READ THE OUTPUT BEFORE YOU WAIT. This is the one way to deadlock with this module.**
//
// A pipe has a finite buffer — 4KB on Linux by default, 64KB typical on Windows. A child
// that produces more than that blocks inside `write` until somebody drains the other end.
// A parent sitting in `wait` will never drain it. Neither side is buggy; together they are
// stuck forever, and the program looks hung rather than wrong.
//
// So the order is: `close_input` (so the child sees EOF and can finish), then `capture` or
// a `read_output` loop until it reports EOF, and only THEN `wait`. `capture` does the first
// two, which is why it exists — the safe sequence should be the short one to write.
//
// Deadlocking on the WRITE side is the same hazard mirrored: `write_input` blocks once the
// child's stdin buffer fills and the child is not reading. A protocol that sends more than
// a buffer's worth before reading a reply needs two threads or a poll, and this module
// deliberately provides neither — see the header.

// Is this child holding a pipe the parent can still use?
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn has_input(read c: Child) -> bool { return c.inp != SPROC_NO_PIPE }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn has_output(read c: Child) -> bool { return c.outp != SPROC_NO_PIPE }

@cfg(windows) fn make_pipe(rd: *mut i64, wr: *mut i64, parent_end_is_read: bool) -> bool {
    // `bInheritHandle` TRUE, so the pair CAN be inherited; then the parent's own end is
    // made non-inheritable below. Both steps matter: without the first the child gets
    // nothing, and without the second the child inherits the parent's end too, keeps it
    // open, and the parent's drain loop never sees EOF.
    var sa: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, SPROC_SA_LEN)
    zero_bytes(sa, SPROC_SA_LEN)
    let sw: *mut u32 = sa as *mut u32
    unsafe { (sw + 0).* = SPROC_SA_LEN as u32 }
    unsafe { (sw + SPROC_SA_INHERIT_WORD).* = 1 as u32 }

    var pr: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, 1)
    var pw: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, 1)
    unsafe { (pr + 0).* = 0 }
    unsafe { (pw + 0).* = 0 }
    let rc: i32 = CreatePipe(pr as cptr, pw as cptr, sa as cptr, 0)
    var r: i64 = 0
    var w: i64 = 0
    unsafe { r = (pr + 0).* }
    unsafe { w = (pw + 0).* }
    free_ptr(pw)
    free_ptr(pr)
    free_ptr(sa)
    if rc == 0 { return false }

    if parent_end_is_read {
        SetHandleInformation(r as cptr, SPROC_HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT, 0)
    } else {
        SetHandleInformation(w as cptr, SPROC_HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT, 0)
    }
    unsafe { (rd + 0).* = r }
    unsafe { (wr + 0).* = w }
    return true
}

// Start `cmd` with its stdin and stdout connected to this process.
//
// The same command semantics as `start` — the platform shell — and the same capability
// gate. What is different is that the parent can now talk to the child and read its
// answer, which is what a long-lived plugin server needs and what `system()` made
// impossible.
//
// **stderr goes to the same pipe as stdout**, deliberately: a second pipe is a second
// thing to drain, and draining two of them from one thread without a poll is the deadlock
// above with extra steps. A caller who needs them apart can redirect in the command.
@cfg(windows) pub fn start_piped(mut s: Spawner, read cmd: str) -> Child !{ SysProcFailed(i32), SysProcRefused } {
    if s.allowed == false {
        s.refused = s.refused + 1
        return err(SysProcRefused)
    }

    // Four one-element slots rather than two pairs: `oh + 1` is raw-pointer arithmetic,
    // and the unsafe ladder refuses it outside an `unsafe` block. Widening the block to
    // cover a call would have made `unsafe` mean "and also this function", which is the
    // opposite of what the extent is for.
    var oh_r: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, 1)
    var oh_w: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, 1)
    var ih_r: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, 1)
    var ih_w: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, 1)
    unsafe { (oh_r + 0).* = 0 }
    unsafe { (oh_w + 0).* = 0 }
    unsafe { (ih_r + 0).* = 0 }
    unsafe { (ih_w + 0).* = 0 }

    var ok_out: bool = make_pipe(oh_r, oh_w, true)
    var ok_in: bool = false
    if ok_out { ok_in = make_pipe(ih_r, ih_w, false) }

    var out_rd: i64 = 0
    var out_wr: i64 = 0
    var in_rd: i64 = 0
    var in_wr: i64 = 0
    unsafe { out_rd = (oh_r + 0).* }
    unsafe { out_wr = (oh_w + 0).* }
    unsafe { in_rd = (ih_r + 0).* }
    unsafe { in_wr = (ih_w + 0).* }
    free_ptr(ih_w)
    free_ptr(ih_r)
    free_ptr(oh_w)
    free_ptr(oh_r)

    if ok_out == false or ok_in == false {
        let code: i32 = syserr.last_raw()
        // A half-made pair still has to be given back.
        if ok_out {
            CloseHandle(out_rd as cptr)
            CloseHandle(out_wr as cptr)
        }
        return err(SysProcFailed(code))
    }

    var line: *mut u8 = nul_join("cmd.exe /c ", cmd)
    var si: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, SPROC_STARTUPINFO_LEN)
    var pi: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, SPROC_PROCESS_INFO_LEN)
    zero_bytes(si, SPROC_STARTUPINFO_LEN)
    zero_bytes(pi, SPROC_PROCESS_INFO_LEN)
    let sw: *mut u32 = si as *mut u32
    unsafe { (sw + 0).* = SPROC_STARTUPINFO_LEN as u32 }
    // Without STARTF_USESTDHANDLES the three handle fields are ignored entirely and the
    // child quietly inherits the parent's console instead — which looks like it worked.
    unsafe { (sw + SPROC_SI_FLAGS_WORD).* = SPROC_STARTF_USESTDHANDLES }
    let ss: *mut i64 = si as *mut i64
    unsafe { (ss + SPROC_SI_STDIN_SLOT).* = in_rd }
    unsafe { (ss + SPROC_SI_STDOUT_SLOT).* = out_wr }
    unsafe { (ss + SPROC_SI_STDERR_SLOT).* = out_wr }

    // `bInheritHandles` TRUE — the whole point, and the flag `start` passes as 0.
    let rc: i32 = CreateProcessA(null, line as cptr, null, null, 1, 0, null, null, si as cptr, pi as cptr)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if rc == 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }

    var h: i64 = 0
    var th: i64 = 0
    var pid: i64 = 0
    if rc != 0 {
        let ps: *mut i64 = pi as *mut i64
        unsafe { h = (ps + SPROC_PI_HPROCESS_SLOT).* }
        unsafe { th = (ps + SPROC_PI_HTHREAD_SLOT).* }
        let pw: *mut u32 = pi as *mut u32
        var raw: u32 = 0
        unsafe { raw = (pw + SPROC_PI_PID_WORD).* }
        pid = raw as i64
    }

    free_ptr(pi)
    free_ptr(si)
    free_ptr(line)

    // **The parent closes the CHILD's ends immediately**, whether or not the spawn worked.
    // Holding the write end of the child's stdout would keep that pipe open from this
    // side, so the parent's own read would never see EOF — the classic hang.
    CloseHandle(out_wr as cptr)
    CloseHandle(in_rd as cptr)

    if rc == 0 {
        CloseHandle(out_rd as cptr)
        CloseHandle(in_wr as cptr)
        return err(SysProcFailed(code))
    }

    s.started = s.started + 1
    return ok(Child{ h: h, th: th, pid: pid, live: true, done: false, state: SPROC_RUNNING, code: 0, inp: in_wr, outp: out_rd })
}

@cfg(posix) pub fn start_piped(mut s: Spawner, read cmd: str) -> Child !{ SysProcFailed(i32), SysProcRefused } {
    if s.allowed == false {
        s.refused = s.refused + 1
        return err(SysProcRefused)
    }

    // Two pipes, each `{ read fd, write fd }`.
    var op: *mut i32 = alloc(i32, 2)
    var ip: *mut i32 = alloc(i32, 2)
    if pipe(op as cptr) != 0 {
        let c1: i32 = syserr.last_raw()
        free_ptr(ip)
        free_ptr(op)
        return err(SysProcFailed(c1))
    }
    if pipe(ip as cptr) != 0 {
        let c2: i32 = syserr.last_raw()
        var o0: i32 = 0
        var o1: i32 = 0
        unsafe { o0 = (op + 0).* }
        unsafe { o1 = (op + 1).* }
        sys_close(o0 as i64)
        sys_close(o1 as i64)
        free_ptr(ip)
        free_ptr(op)
        return err(SysProcFailed(c2))
    }
    var out_rd: i64 = 0
    var out_wr: i64 = 0
    var in_rd: i64 = 0
    var in_wr: i64 = 0
    var t: i32 = 0
    unsafe { t = (op + 0).* }  out_rd = t as i64
    unsafe { t = (op + 1).* }  out_wr = t as i64
    unsafe { t = (ip + 0).* }  in_rd = t as i64
    unsafe { t = (ip + 1).* }  in_wr = t as i64
    free_ptr(ip)
    free_ptr(op)

    // argv is built BEFORE the fork: allocating in the child between fork and exec is
    // exactly the async-signal-unsafe thing that makes `fork` dangerous.
    var shp: *mut u8 = nul_copy("/bin/sh")
    var a0: *mut u8 = nul_copy("sh")
    var a1: *mut u8 = nul_copy("-c")
    var a2: *mut u8 = nul_copy(cmd)
    var argv: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, 4)
    unsafe { (argv + 0).* = a0 as i64 }
    unsafe { (argv + 1).* = a1 as i64 }
    unsafe { (argv + 2).* = a2 as i64 }
    unsafe { (argv + 3).* = 0 }

    let pid: i64 = fork()
    if pid == 0 {
        // **The child.** Everything from here to `execv` is async-signal-safe: `dup2`,
        // `close`, `execv`, `_exit`. No allocation, no library state.
        sys_close(out_rd)
        sys_close(in_wr)
        dup2(in_rd, 0)
        dup2(out_wr, 1)
        dup2(out_wr, 2)
        sys_close(in_rd)
        sys_close(out_wr)
        execv(shp as cptr, argv as cptr)
        // Only reachable if exec FAILED. 127 is the shell's own "command not found",
        // which is the closest thing to a convention here.
        sys_exit(127)
    }

    free_ptr(argv)
    free_ptr(a2)
    free_ptr(a1)
    free_ptr(a0)
    free_ptr(shp)

    // The parent closes the child's ends — same reason as the Windows branch.
    sys_close(in_rd)
    sys_close(out_wr)

    if pid < 0 {
        let c3: i32 = syserr.last_raw()
        sys_close(out_rd)
        sys_close(in_wr)
        return err(SysProcFailed(c3))
    }

    s.started = s.started + 1
    return ok(Child{ h: 0, th: 0, pid: pid, live: true, done: false, state: SPROC_RUNNING, code: 0, inp: in_wr, outp: out_rd })
}

// ---- reading and writing --------------------------------------------------------------

@cfg(windows) fn pipe_read(h: i64, buf: *mut u8, n: usize) -> i64 {
    var got: *mut u32 = alloc(u32, 1)
    unsafe { (got + 0).* = 0 }
    let rc: i32 = ReadFile(h as cptr, buf as cptr, n as u32, got as cptr, null)
    var k: u32 = 0
    unsafe { k = (got + 0).* }
    free_ptr(got)
    if rc != 0 { return k as i64 }
    // **EOF is an ERROR return on Windows, not a zero-length read.** A caller that treated
    // ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE as a failure would turn every successful capture into an error at
    // the very last step.
    if syserr.last_raw() == SPROC_ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE { return 0 }
    return 0 - 1
}

@cfg(posix) fn pipe_read(h: i64, buf: *mut u8, n: usize) -> i64 {
    return sys_read(h, buf as cptr, n)
}

@cfg(windows) fn pipe_write(h: i64, buf: *mut u8, n: usize) -> i64 {
    var put: *mut u32 = alloc(u32, 1)
    unsafe { (put + 0).* = 0 }
    let rc: i32 = WriteFile(h as cptr, buf as cptr, n as u32, put as cptr, null)
    var k: u32 = 0
    unsafe { k = (put + 0).* }
    free_ptr(put)
    if rc == 0 { return 0 - 1 }
    return k as i64
}

@cfg(posix) fn pipe_write(h: i64, buf: *mut u8, n: usize) -> i64 {
    return sys_write(h, buf as cptr, n)
}

@cfg(windows) fn pipe_close(h: i64) { CloseHandle(h as cptr) }

@cfg(posix) fn pipe_close(h: i64) { sys_close(h) }

// Read up to `buf.len` bytes of the child's output.
//
// **Zero means EOF** — the child closed its end — and it is not an error. `-1` is a real
// failure. That distinction is the reason this returns a count rather than a bool, and it
// is the same shape `sysnet.recv_bytes` uses for a peer that closed cleanly.
pub fn read_output(mut c: Child, mut buf: []u8) -> i64 {
    if c.outp == SPROC_NO_PIPE { return 0 - 1 }
    if buf.len == 0 { return 0 }
    var scratch: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, buf.len)
    let got: i64 = pipe_read(c.outp, scratch, buf.len)
    if got > 0 {
        var i: usize = 0
        for i < got as usize {
            var b: u8 = 0
            unsafe { b = (scratch + i).* }
            buf[i] = b
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    free_ptr(scratch)
    return got
}

// Write `data` to the child's input. Returns bytes written, or -1.
//
// A SHORT write is possible and is not an error: the pipe's buffer filled. `write_all`
// is the loop, and it is separate for the same reason `sysnet` separates them — a caller
// that must not block indefinitely wants to see the short write.
pub fn write_input(mut c: Child, read data: []u8) -> i64 {
    if c.inp == SPROC_NO_PIPE { return 0 - 1 }
    if data.len == 0 { return 0 }
    var scratch: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, data.len)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < data.len {
        unsafe { (scratch + i).* = data[i] }
        i = i + 1
    }
    let put: i64 = pipe_write(c.inp, scratch, data.len)
    free_ptr(scratch)
    return put
}

// Keep writing until everything is gone. Returns what was written.
pub fn write_all(mut c: Child, read data: []u8) -> i64 {
    var off: usize = 0
    for off < data.len {
        let n: i64 = write_input(c, data[off .. data.len])
        if n <= 0 { return off as i64 }
        off = off + n as usize
    }
    return off as i64
}

// Send the child EOF on its stdin.
//
// **Usually required, not optional.** A filter — `sort`, `cat`, `wc` — reads until EOF
// before it produces anything, so a parent that never closes the input waits for output
// that will never come. Idempotent.
pub fn close_input(mut c: Child) {
    if c.inp == SPROC_NO_PIPE { return }
    pipe_close(c.inp)
    c.inp = SPROC_NO_PIPE
}

// Drain the child's output to EOF, as text.
//
// **This is the safe sequence, packaged**: it closes the input first (so a filter can
// finish) and then reads until EOF, which is exactly the order that avoids the deadlock
// this section opens with. Call it BEFORE `wait`.
//
// Returns what was read; a child that produced nothing gives an empty `String`, which is
// not an error.
pub fn capture(mut c: Child) -> String {
    var s: String = string_new()
    close_input(c)
    if c.outp == SPROC_NO_PIPE { return s }
    var scratch: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, SPROC_PIPE_CHUNK)
    var going: bool = true
    for going {
        let got: i64 = pipe_read(c.outp, scratch, SPROC_PIPE_CHUNK)
        if got <= 0 {
            going = false
        } else {
            let chunk: []u8 = slice(u8, scratch, got as usize)
            string_push(s, from_utf8(chunk))
        }
    }
    free_ptr(scratch)
    pipe_close(c.outp)
    c.outp = SPROC_NO_PIPE
    return s
}

// ---- the two platform primitives ----------------------------------------------------
//
// Everything above `wait_timeout` is written once and these are the only split parts:
// "has it finished, and with what". Keeping the split this small is what makes the
// polling policy the same code on both platforms rather than two policies that agree
// today.

// Has `c` finished? `SPROC_RUNNING` while it is alive; otherwise the real outcome.
// Reports `SPROC_RUNNING` for a child whose state cannot be read at all, because a
// caller's bounded wait must still terminate on its own timeout rather than on a lie.
@cfg(posix) fn poll_once(read c: Child) -> Status {
    var st: *mut i32 = alloc(i32, 1)
    unsafe { (st + 0).* = 0 }
    let got: i64 = waitpid(c.pid, st as cptr, SPROC_WNOHANG)
    var raw: i32 = 0
    unsafe { raw = (st + 0).* }
    free_ptr(st)

    // 0 means "alive, nothing to report". Negative means the wait itself failed — most
    // often ECHILD, because something already reaped it — and there is no honest status
    // to give, so it is reported as an exit with code 0 rather than as running forever.
    if got == 0 { return Status{ state: SPROC_RUNNING, code: 0 } }
    if got < 0 { return Status{ state: SPROC_EXITED, code: 0 } }
    return decode_wait_status(raw)
}

// POSIX wait-status decoding. `WIFEXITED` and friends are macros, so they are not
// bindable and the bit layout is spelled out here instead. Low seven bits: the signal
// that killed it, zero if it exited normally. Next eight: the exit code.
@no_alloc @no_os fn decode_wait_status(raw: i32) -> Status {
    let sig: i32 = raw & SPROC_STATUS_SIGMASK
    if sig == 0 {
        let code: i32 = (raw >> SPROC_STATUS_CODESHIFT) & SPROC_STATUS_CODEMASK
        return Status{ state: SPROC_EXITED, code: code }
    }
    return Status{ state: SPROC_SIGNALLED, code: sig }
}

@cfg(windows) fn poll_once(read c: Child) -> Status {
    // `WaitForSingleObject(h, 0)` and NOT `GetExitCodeProcess`: STILL_ACTIVE is 259, so a
    // child that exits with 259 is indistinguishable from a running one through the exit
    // code alone. This call has no in-band value to collide with. See the header.
    let w: i32 = WaitForSingleObject(c.h as cptr, 0)
    if w == SPROC_WAIT_TIMEOUT { return Status{ state: SPROC_RUNNING, code: 0 } }
    if w != SPROC_WAIT_SIGNALED { return Status{ state: SPROC_EXITED, code: 0 } }

    var cw: *mut u32 = alloc(u32, 1)
    unsafe { (cw + 0).* = 0 }
    let rc: i32 = GetExitCodeProcess(c.h as cptr, cw as cptr)
    var raw: u32 = 0
    unsafe { raw = (cw + 0).* }
    free_ptr(cw)
    if rc == 0 { return Status{ state: SPROC_EXITED, code: 0 } }

    // A Windows child has no signals; a terminated one is reported through whatever exit
    // code the terminator chose, which is why `terminate` picks a fixed one.
    if raw == SPROC_KILL_CODE { return Status{ state: SPROC_SIGNALLED, code: SPROC_SIGKILL } }
    return Status{ state: SPROC_EXITED, code: raw as i32 }
}

// Block until `c` finishes, using the platform's own blocking wait. Not a poll: there is
// nothing to gain from waking up early when the caller has said it will wait as long as
// it takes.
@cfg(posix) fn block_once(read c: Child) -> Status {
    var st: *mut i32 = alloc(i32, 1)
    unsafe { (st + 0).* = 0 }
    let got: i64 = waitpid(c.pid, st as cptr, 0)
    var raw: i32 = 0
    unsafe { raw = (st + 0).* }
    free_ptr(st)
    if got < 0 { return Status{ state: SPROC_EXITED, code: 0 } }
    return decode_wait_status(raw)
}

@cfg(windows) fn block_once(read c: Child) -> Status {
    // 0xFFFFFFFF is INFINITE. Written as a `u32` maximum rather than as `-1` so the
    // constant reads the way the header does.
    WaitForSingleObject(c.h as cptr, 4294967295 as u32)
    return poll_once(c)
}

@cfg(posix) fn signal_child(read c: Child) -> bool {
    return kill(c.pid, SPROC_SIGKILL) == 0
}

@cfg(windows) fn signal_child(read c: Child) -> bool {
    return TerminateProcess(c.h as cptr, SPROC_KILL_CODE) != 0
}

@cfg(posix) fn close_child(read c: Child) {
    // Nothing to close: a POSIX child is named by a pid, and `wait`/`try_wait` already
    // reaped it. The empty body is the platform difference, stated rather than hidden
    // behind a guard at every call site.
}

@cfg(windows) fn close_child(read c: Child) {
    // BOTH handles. Forgetting `hThread` is the classic CreateProcess leak: the process
    // handle is the one everybody remembers and the thread handle is the one that keeps
    // the kernel object alive.
    if c.h != 0 { CloseHandle(c.h as cptr) }
    if c.th != 0 { CloseHandle(c.th as cptr) }
}

// ---- waiting -----------------------------------------------------------------------

// Has it finished YET? Returns immediately, whatever the answer.
//
// The status is cached, so a caller may ask repeatedly and a POSIX child is reaped
// exactly once however many times this is called.
pub fn try_wait(mut c: Child) -> Status {
    if c.done { return Status{ state: c.state, code: c.code } }
    if c.live == false { return Status{ state: SPROC_EXITED, code: 0 } }
    let s: Status = poll_once(c)
    if s.state != SPROC_RUNNING {
        c.done = true
        c.state = s.state
        c.code = s.code
    }
    return s
}

// Block until it finishes. Never reports `SPROC_RUNNING`.
pub fn wait(mut c: Child) -> Status {
    if c.done { return Status{ state: c.state, code: c.code } }
    if c.live == false { return Status{ state: SPROC_EXITED, code: 0 } }
    let s: Status = block_once(c)
    c.done = true
    c.state = s.state
    c.code = s.code
    return s
}

// **Wait up to `timeout_nanos`.** The call this module exists to provide: it returns
// `SPROC_RUNNING` when the child outlived the budget, and that is a normal answer rather
// than an error — a caller that wanted to give up now knows it may.
//
// `clk` is taken rather than read from the OS so a test can drive the timeout with
// `time.manual()` and assert on the loop without waiting for real seconds. Under a manual
// clock nothing sleeps, so the loop spins the clock forward; under `time.host()` it
// sleeps and the elapsed time is real. Both are the same code.
//
// A non-positive timeout is one liveness check and no sleeping, which makes
// `wait_timeout(c, clk, 0)` a spelling of `try_wait` that a caller composing timeouts
// does not have to special-case.
pub fn wait_timeout(mut c: Child, mut clk: time.Clock, timeout_nanos: i64) -> Status {
    if c.done { return Status{ state: c.state, code: c.code } }
    if c.live == false { return Status{ state: SPROC_EXITED, code: 0 } }

    var waited: i64 = 0
    var s: Status = poll_once(c)
    for s.state == SPROC_RUNNING {
        if waited >= timeout_nanos { break }
        var slice: i64 = SPROC_POLL_NANOS
        let left: i64 = timeout_nanos - waited
        if slice > left { slice = left }
        // The clock reports what it ACTUALLY waited, which on Windows is routinely more
        // than asked for. Adding the request instead would make a loop that believes it
        // has waited a millisecond when 15 have passed.
        waited = waited + time.wait(clk, slice)
        s = poll_once(c)
    }

    if s.state != SPROC_RUNNING {
        c.done = true
        c.state = s.state
        c.code = s.code
    }
    return s
}

// ---- ending ------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Kill it. Returns whether the platform accepted the request — NOT whether the child is
// gone, which is a different question and is answered by waiting afterwards.
//
// `SIGKILL` rather than `SIGTERM` on purpose: this is the call a timeout reaches for, and
// a child that ignores `SIGTERM` is exactly the child that made the timeout fire. A
// polite shutdown is a protocol, and a protocol belongs above this line.
pub fn terminate(mut c: Child) -> bool {
    if c.live == false { return false }
    if c.done { return false }
    return signal_child(c)
}

// Give the handles back. Idempotent, so it composes with a caller that is not sure —
// the `std/list` teardown contract.
//
// **Call it exactly once per Child VALUE, not per copy.** `Child` is freely copyable and
// the language cannot yet say otherwise (see the header); releasing through two copies
// closes a handle the platform may already have reissued to something else.
pub fn release(mut c: Child) {
    // Pipes first, and OUTSIDE the `live` guard: `capture` may already have released the
    // process handles through an earlier path, and a descriptor left open is a leak the
    // `live` flag does not describe. Both closers are idempotent.
    close_input(c)
    if c.outp != SPROC_NO_PIPE {
        pipe_close(c.outp)
        c.outp = SPROC_NO_PIPE
    }
    if c.live == false { return }
    close_child(c)
    c.live = false
}

// Wait up to `timeout_nanos`, kill it if it is still running, then reap and release.
//
// The composition every caller of a bounded wait actually wants, written once because
// getting it wrong leaks a process: a `terminate` without a following `wait` leaves a
// zombie on POSIX, since the kernel keeps the entry until somebody collects it.
pub fn wait_or_kill(mut c: Child, mut clk: time.Clock, timeout_nanos: i64) -> Status {
    var s: Status = wait_timeout(c, clk, timeout_nanos)
    if s.state == SPROC_RUNNING {
        terminate(c)
        s = wait(c)
    }
    release(c)
    return s
}
